WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.543
Look to your left.

00:00:01.543 --> 00:00:02.752
Now, to your right.

00:00:02.752 --> 00:00:05.588
Wherever you are,
whatever you're doing, it's safe

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:08.091
to say you're surrounded by matter.

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:12.846
That's because matter makes up
just about everything in existence,

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:17.475
from stars and galaxies
to planets to puppies and pizza.

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:21.646
But matter has a rarely seen
rival: antimatter.

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:25.859
For every type of matter particle,
there is an antimatter doppelganger

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.654
with the same mass
but the opposite electric charge.

00:00:29.654 --> 00:00:33.616
An electron has a positively charged
counterpart called a positron.

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.911
A proton's antiparticle is an antiproton.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.580
You get the idea.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.874
So why did matter win out?

00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:44.002
Why aren't there
puppies made of antimatter?

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.629
The universe didn't have to be this way.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:50.258
The Big Bang is thought to have made
matter and antimatter in equal amounts.

00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:52.802
These opposites annihilate when they meet.

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:57.766
So you'd expect the universe
to be a bleak, lifeless wasteland

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:02.228
filled with nothing but energy,
but somehow matter won out.

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:06.066
I'm going to tell you a story
about how a tiny, unassuming particle,

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:08.651
the neutrino, and its hypothetical
relatives

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:13.615
could explain the existence of, well,
everything.

00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:16.826
[VIDEO GAME MUSIC] 

00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:21.623
Neutrinos are super weird
subatomic particles.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.125
I'm kind of obsessed with them.

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:29.380
They have ridiculously tiny masses,
and they're often called ghost particles

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.383
because they are so ethereal
that they can pass through

00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:35.470
the entire earth without leaving a trace.

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:40.475
There are more neutrinos than any other
particle with mass that we know of.

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:43.645
They are produced
in the sun, in radioactive decay

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:46.272
and in other reactions
throughout the cosmos.

00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:49.275
But their stealth makes them
very hard to study.

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:53.655
For something so common,
we know shockingly little about them.

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:58.576
And these mysterious particles
might be even stranger still.

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:02.455
Many physicists suspect
that in the early universe,

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.750
heavy relatives of today's neutrinos
proliferated.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:11.172
Those early neutrinos, the origin story
goes, helped bring about the matter glut.

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:13.299
OK, stick with me.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.302
It gets complicated,
and it might sound counterintuitive.

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:20.098
It turns out in order to create today's matter,

00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:23.368
you have to first create extra antimatter.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.645
[EXPLOSION SOUND] 

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:28.273
Here's how scientists
think it might have gone down.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:31.276
The heavy neutrinos decayed
into antimatter

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:34.863
particles more often than they decayed
into matter particles.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.992
From there, a physics process
called a sphaleron possible

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:41.744
only in the newbie universe took over.

00:02:42.036 --> 00:02:45.707
Like a cosmic cheat code,
the sphaleron process converted

00:02:45.707 --> 00:02:50.128
the newly formed excess of antimatter
into an excess of matter,

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:55.508
specifically quarks, the type of particle
that makes up protons and neutrons.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.761
So to recap,

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:01.055
heavy neutrinos made extra antimatter.

00:03:01.431 --> 00:03:04.058
Then that extra antimatter
flipped into matter.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.730
And that's how many physicists suspect
we got the matter filled universe

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:09.856
we see today.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:13.359
But scientists don't know if that's
what really went down.

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:18.323
This all would have happened at conditions
too extreme to reproduce in a lab.

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:20.825
So how can scientists confirm the story?

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:23.411
That's where today's neutrinos come in.

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:28.541
If the heavy neutrinos really did decay
unevenly into matter and antimatter,

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:32.086
then physics probably also treats
today's neutrinos

00:03:32.086 --> 00:03:35.882
differently than their antimatter
particles, antineutrinos.

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:39.969
So scientists are looking to modern
neutrinos for clues.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:43.848
Researchers are focused on
one of neutrinos' strangest

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:46.851
abilities: changing their identities.

00:03:47.435 --> 00:03:52.607
Neutrinos come in a menu of three flavors:
electron, muon and tau.

00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:54.734
But these flavors aren't locked in.

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.487
Neutrinos oscillate between flavors.

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:01.241
A particle might be born
as an electron neutrino,

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:05.286
but when scientists measure it,
they might find a muon neutrino instead.

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:08.498
Scientists want to measure
how often neutrinos oscillate

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:11.292
and compare
that with antineutrino oscillations.

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:14.587
If the two don't match,
that means physics does

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:17.548
treat neutrinos
and antineutrinos differently.

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:21.511
That matter-antimatter
mismatch is something physicists call

00:04:21.511 --> 00:04:26.641
charge-parity violation or CP violation,
and it's a necessary ingredient

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:30.395
in the story of how matter came
to dominate the universe.

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:34.482
To catch enough of these ghostly particles
to compare how neutrinos

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:39.070
and antineutrinos oscillate,
physicists are building giant detectors.

00:04:39.821 --> 00:04:43.491
The Deep Underground Neutrino Experiment
or DUNE

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:47.245
in an old mine in South
Dakota will spot neutrinos

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:50.415
interacting
with the liquid argon inside the detector,

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.042
allowing scientists
to determine the flavors.

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:58.256
DUNE ultimately plans to use a detector
so big that the liquid inside

00:04:58.256 --> 00:05:01.092
could fill nearly 20 Olympic
swimming pools.

00:05:01.551 --> 00:05:05.346
Another neutrino detector 
called Hyper-Kamiokande in Japan

00:05:05.346 --> 00:05:11.019
will also be huge, filled
with 260,000 metric tons of water.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:16.774
It will be lined with 40,000 light sensors
that will spot dim flashes produced

00:05:16.774 --> 00:05:19.027
when the neutrinos interact
with the water.

00:05:19.944 --> 00:05:23.656
Both detectors are being built
deep underground, using the earth

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:27.660
above to filter out other particles
that can interfere with measurements.

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:32.707
Particle accelerators will create
beams of neutrinos to be sent across

00:05:32.707 --> 00:05:36.461
hundreds of kilometers,
giving them plenty of time to oscillate.

00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:40.298
The scientists will compare
the neutrinos' oscillations

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:43.176
with those of antineutrinos
sent on the same trek.

00:05:43.634 --> 00:05:47.221
If the neutrino and antineutrino
oscillations don't match,

00:05:47.263 --> 00:05:50.975
it's a hint that the heavy neutrino story
could be true.

00:05:51.559 --> 00:05:53.603
But it wouldn't be the last word.

00:05:53.603 --> 00:05:56.522
Neutrinos are a particle
that's close to my heart.

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:57.190
Literally.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.901
Trillions of them are streaming
through my body

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:01.778
and through yours at every moment.

00:06:01.819 --> 00:06:04.155
As they speed through me,
I like to imagine

00:06:04.155 --> 00:06:07.909
giving them a word of thanks,
possibly for all of existence.

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:11.996
You should have had a burrito beforehand
[laughs].

00:06:11.996 --> 00:06:13.873
Ehhh.

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:18.378
[laughs] I should have gotten you a burrito.

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:21.005
Actually, my burrito wasn't very good,
so never mind.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.132
OK. What was I saying?

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:26.302
Be sure to like and subscribe
to see more videos like this one.

